  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.17.0 — MINOR on the 0.x line, the repository's signal for caller-observable change.\n\nTWO merged engine commits since 0.16.0.\n\nREFUSAL SPELLINGS (#1573): refusal text is now derived from the command-contract flag spelling table,\nincluding the non-long `-n` limit spelling. Contract output and a refusal can no longer silently name\ndifferent flags.\n\nSAFE ROTATED-SESSION RECOVERY (#1857): a live claim carrying this worker id but a different session\nstill refuses — the #419 twin guard is not weakened — but its diagnostic now names the marker session\nand the `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`/`FSGG_WORKER` pin needed for a worker whose harness rotated sessions.\nIt explicitly warns not to mint a new id and strand the live claim.\n\nPatch is not defensible: both changes alter caller-visible diagnostic/recovery behavior on a 0.x tool.\nNo Protocol.fs wire-surface change is included.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.16.0):\n\nEIGHT merged engine commits since 0.15.0 across 32 source/project files. The #1900 squash commit\nconsolidates four reviewed input commits, making eleven pre-merge inputs in total. Protocol.fs moved,\nso the generated worker contract and the published engine were out of step until this release.\n\nFOLLOW-UP QUEUES AND THE DONE BOUNDARY (.github#1900/#1952): `followup audit` now observes every local\nqueue, previews reconciliation, and applies safe removal only after a durable GitHub disposition is\nverified. `done` audits residual promises before stamping completion. `--apply` is now valid for the\nfollowup command, and the JSON/text result vocabulary gains the audit and reconciliation outcomes.\n\nFAIL-CLOSED MARKER SCANS (.github#1896/#1949): scheduler selection, claim/adopt/reap checks, and active\ntouch-set decisions no longer continue from a partial comment scan. A previously successful operation\nmay now refuse with an incomplete/undetermined result rather than silently ignoring a hidden claim.\n\nSCHEDULING AND RANKING: BLOCKER-CLEARED respects an in-flight implementation (.github#1738/#1921);\ndecision items stay out of the worker queue (.github#1887/#1946); Severity is an ordered rank axis\n(.github#1901); and `lint` names consolidation candidates when overlapping work is one cause\n(.github#1915).\n\nSKILL AND KIT DIAGNOSTICS: skill views are derived from receiver declarations (.github#1948), and KIT\nDIGEST gives correct claim advice for skill files (.github#1942).\n\nKNOWN RESIDUE: .github#1892 still decides whether secondary rate limits receive a distinct exit code or\nremain EX_RATE with richer data. .github#1857 still tracks a session id rotating mid-item and stranding\nthe worker's own heartbeat/release/done operations. Neither behavior is claimed fixed by this release.",
